> With the scenario you describe, you'll need to live with the assumption
> that the g(0) you estimated for a portion of your dataset applies to the
> entire dataset.  To adjust your density estimate derived from the single
> platform survey, accounting for the g(0)<1, you would do this:
>
>

> using the estimate of g(0) as what the literature calls a "multiplier".
>
> The harder part of the adjustment is to incorporate the uncertainty in
> your estimate of g(0) into your adjusted estimate.  If we generically
> refer to an estimate of a multiplier as "c", then this formula for
> variance in density comes into play:
>
>

> where the first term is the encounter rate coefficient of variation, the
> second term is coefficient of variation for the detection function and
> the third term is the coefficient of variation for your estimate of g(0).
>
> Multipliers and their use in adjusting estimates, along with properly
> propagating uncertainty, is described in more detail in Buckland et al.
> (2001) Section 6.2.3
>
> It is also the case that multipliers have been programmed into the
> graphical interface version of Distance.  Consult Chapter 8,
> Conventional distance sampling analysis--section entitled "Multipliers
> in CDS analysis" for further details.
